# RpcGuard round 3 (review m2): heap a connection thread takes to build a -32021 busy answer
#
# Scratch unit test zz_scratch_busy_peaks (source below; added to src/rpc.rs's test module only for these two runs,
# never committed), run with `cargo test --release --locked --offline --lib zz_scratch_busy_peaks -- --nocapture`
# under the CPU lock, crate::alloc_meter::peak (requested heap, per thread, relative to the call).
# Per id shape: batchPeak = a 20-member batch whose members all carry that id; singlePeak = one request;
# answer = answer bytes; kept = the id was echoed (not null). A batch over JSON_VALUES_MAX is answered with one null-id
# error unparsed (answer=112).
#
# BEFORE: the busy path as at fa97594 (reply_32021, scan_ids and obj_id unchanged from fa97594 at the time of the
# run; ids kept as parsed serde_json::Value trees). AFTER: the round-3 busy path (ids kept as their <= 128 B
# encodings; id_encoding parses only ids that can fit). The committed test a_busy_answer_allocates_no_parse_tree
# asserts every adversarial shape within SMALL_REPLY_PEAK = 132,768 B and prints BUSY-PEAK (the round-3 suite logs).
#
# Worst before: nestObj25 (20 ids, each 25 nested objects, 126 B): 340,378 B for one answer, against the 5,184 B
# (SMALL_REPLY_MAX) per connection the round-2 A13 term counted. Worst after: 24,028 B here; 47,627 B over the
# committed test's shapes (objects63 as a singleton).
#
